A||||11||Coop88||Sanding question||||1||
Z||000000||Coop88||08-11-2001||04:23 PM||||I need to sand the sunroof body panel from my parts car. It has original paint with heavy oxidation. I did a search and found out people use anywhere from 60 to 320 grit. Would anything between these two grits work for the oxidation? After I remove the oxidation, do I need to sand with anything else? I'll be doing this by hand with a sanding block.  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Mach10||08-11-2001||04:51 PM||||Pepends what you want to do with it. If it's just the clearcoat peeling up, then there should be no need to strip the panel down to the material itself. I would recommend a 320grit paper for the rough stuff, as it won't leave gouges that need to be filled with glazing putty, but still strip fast enough to make the job less painful. After that, I'd run over it a few times with 400 grit, to make it nice and smooth. 60 grit is VERY coarse, and will eat through the paint quickly, but leave scratches that will show up on the new paint unless filled with a compound, or else sanded down with a finer paper. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||08-11-2001||06:51 PM||||I finish sand everthing with 400 before i paint. 320 would be ok to start, it would cut faster. Id also put a primer coat on it and lightly sand with 400 before final color.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||08-11-2001||09:23 PM||||As soft as Fiero panels are.... you can strip the paint to the substrate really quick and dig nasty cuts/holes fast.<P>If you are after removing trashed clear coat... start at 400 and go finer from there. Use it wet. It works faster and doesn't plug the paper. Keep it wet. keep paper in one hand and a spray bottle or water hose in the other.<P>Go more coarse only if you really need to. It's better to cut slow than dig a hole or make big scratches. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||XDrewX||08-12-2001||01:23 PM||||I am about to remove the paint from the roof, mine is cracking off in dime size pieces, it seems to have started at all 4 corners of the sunroof and is spreading, it starts as a bubble under the paint.<BR>i also noticed my front hood is getting a bubble under the paint right near the headlight. My rear hood is fine.<BR>This is supposed to be the original paint, according to the original owner.<P>I am going to take the paint down the the fiberglass, it chips so easily i may just pick it off.  <P>I was wondering what caused this, and will i have this problem after i repaint the roof ???<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||08-12-2001||01:32 PM||||The problem that causes delamination of the paint is often a pin hole chip. The edges of the chip let water under the paint where it heat cycles and lifts the paint more and more. There is nothing that will stop it except Carefully sanding away the effected area. The has to be done very carefully or you'll dig a hole in the panel. The Fiero body panels are clost the same hardness as the paint.<P>Don't try to strip the whole panel. you can easily end up with a wavy panel. ugly ugly...<P>Like metal panels, you want to feather out the chips over large areas. then scuff all the rest, removing any dead clear coat in the process.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mach10||08-12-2001||04:07 PM||||My suggestion, if you are really serious about stripping would be to sand everything until the primer shows through using a very large block. Use it WET. I didn't mention earlier, 'cos I wouldn't even CONSIDER doing bodywork with a dry pad. Use the large block, it'll prevent waviness. My favorite is an 18x3 body sanding tool. Looks almost like a wood plane, only bigger. That'll keep things straight. You should be able to do ok with a 12x3 or similar. Be sure to work in circles. That will keep it smooth. Do this with a 320 grit, then lay a coat of GOOD urethane primer-sealer. Sand with a 400, coat again, sand, coat. Make sure that cures fully, then do your final primer, then topcoat.<P>EDIT: Make sure that you wash and dry things between wet-sandings. I even run an old hairdryer to make sure! <p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 08-12-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000007||Galen||08-12-2001||08:35 PM||||What Ogre said about digging holes into the panels quickly is very true. I know this unfortunate fact very well. I did that wickedly to my hood(s).. hehe  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> but oh well.. I got a parts car<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="purple">Ray</FONT> <FONT COLOR="gray">-</FONT> <FONT COLOR="purple">"With one drop of blood he realized his own mortallity, it was then he saw the true face of God. For he is not someone watching down on us, but he is in us." - Galen 2001</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||08-12-2001||09:02 PM||||Ive stripped many a car in my shop with bad clearcoat. some I just power wash off for about $10 a car, some with a single edge razor blade. start at one edge and go all way to other just like shaving. saves a ton of time and sandpaper. I stripped a whole Ford Taurus at the wash in about 1/2 hour. bare all except the bumpers. I did have some pics of a Neon I stripped in a couple hours with razor blade, but cant find em now.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Coop88||08-12-2001||09:11 PM||||I have a deep groove on the passenger side of the panel that needs to be filled. Will any fiberglass filler work? Or should I use something else? Thanks for the help so far.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Coop88||08-12-2001||11:09 PM||||I started sanding and was wondering what the panel should look like when I've sanded enough. The water is coming off white, so I figure this is the clear coat since I have black paint. Right now the panel is light grey with black spots where the oxidation was. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||artherd||08-13-2001||08:41 AM||||Your intuition is correct, when you start to see the water turn black, then you have *started* to hit the colour coat...<P>At that point, when dry at least, you will be able to see where there is clear left.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
